WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.200
How much weight do you need for the dive?

00:00:03.051 --> 00:00:05.331
The answer comes from a balanced rig.

00:00:06.144 --> 00:00:09.124
A rig is balanced when it passes two checks.

00:00:10.004 --> 00:00:12.924
At the end, you stay at the last stop without effort.

00:00:12.924 --> 00:00:16.194
At the start, you can ascend by swimming if the wing fails.

00:00:17.294 --> 00:00:26.674
For example, our diver weighs eighty kilograms and dives with a single eleven point one litre aluminium tank and a five millimetre wetsuit, in salt water.

00:00:27.298 --> 00:00:30.608
Plus means it floats, and minus means it sinks.

00:00:31.708 --> 00:00:33.528
First, the end of the dive.

00:00:33.528 --> 00:00:39.568
At the three metre stop, with an empty wing and fifty bar left in the tank, you must stay there without effort.

00:00:40.348 --> 00:00:43.308
The wetsuit lifts about three point nine kilograms.

00:00:44.379 --> 00:00:48.859
The air left in the tank weighs about zero point seven kilograms.

00:00:49.946 --> 00:00:54.086
The empty aluminium tank floats: it lifts about two kilograms.

00:00:55.086 --> 00:01:03.946
The regulator weighs about one point four kilograms in water, and the plate, the harness, the wing, the fins and the small items about one point eight kilograms.

00:01:05.005 --> 00:01:12.985
Our diver's body lifts about one point six kilograms, but this is only an estimate, and every body is different.

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:17.590
Now we add lead until this column adds up to zero kilograms.

00:01:18.614 --> 00:01:24.684
Four kilograms of lead weighs about three point six kilograms in water, and our rig is neutral.

00:01:26.784 --> 00:01:33.724
Then the start: at thirty metres with two hundred bar, you must be able to ascend by swimming if the wing fails.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:36.830
The wetsuit is compressed, so it lifts only about one point three kilograms.

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:39.510
The air in the full tank weighs about two point seven kilograms.

00:01:40.052 --> 00:01:43.772
The tank, the regulator, the plate, our diver's body and the lead do not change with depth, so we copy them across.

00:01:44.766 --> 00:01:51.586
So at the start, our rig is about four point six kilograms heavy.

00:01:59.962 --> 00:02:08.402
Most divers can ascend by swimming with a rig about five kilograms heavy, so on paper our rig passes, but only just.

00:02:09.283 --> 00:02:13.343
Test your own limit, because your own check in the water decides.

00:02:14.253 --> 00:02:19.243
If your rig is too heavy, change the gear or carry part of the lead where you can drop it.

00:02:21.343 --> 00:02:23.103
Now use your own numbers.

00:02:23.924 --> 00:02:32.164
At the end of your next dive, do the weight check at the three metre stop, with fifty bar left and an empty wing, and update your table.

00:02:32.989 --> 00:02:37.709
Keep a record of every check, and over time you will see how your weighting changes with your equipment.

00:02:37.869 --> 00:02:46.029
To help you keep that record, Weights Check on iPhone and Diver.Today on the Mac are free on the App Store, with the same data on both.

00:02:47.169 --> 00:02:48.299
diver dot today.
